<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of Freiburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1457">1457</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Albert-Ludwigs-Universität Freiburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of Freiburg is a public university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiburg-im-breisgau">Freiburg im Breisgau</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, founded in 1457 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-vii-archduke-of-austria">Archduke Albert</a> VI of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, then ruler of Further Austria. One of the oldest universities in Germany, it remained Catholic during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> in 1805. Its teachers have included the philosophers Edmund Husserl and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-heidegger">Martin Heidegger</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-freiburg.jpg" alt="University of Freiburg" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: University of Freiburg Vector: Calestyo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siegel_der_Albert-Ludwigs-Universit%C3%A4t_Freiburg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The university began as Albrechts University with four faculties: theology, philosophy, medicine and law. Its foundation belongs to the second wave of university foundings in the German-speaking world in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a> and Basel, and it was the second university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> territory after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. It was established by papal bull, and so, like most medieval universities, was a corporation of the church: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Basel was its chancellor and the bishop of Constance its patron. Its real founder was the sovereign, Archduke Albert VI of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, brother of Emperor Frederick III, who gave it land, endowments and its own jurisdiction. It remained the territorial university until it was handed over to the Habsburgs in 1490.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1679 Freiburg temporarily became French. Louis XIV gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> a free hand in the university, a bilingual programme began on 6 November 1684, and from 1686 to 1698 the faculty fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstanz">Konstanz</a>. After Freiburg was reconquered and made capital of Further Austria, Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>'s reforms of 1767 opened it to students who were not Roman Catholics, added the natural sciences and public administration, and made the university a state institution despite the Church's protests. The suppression of the Jesuits in 1773 ended the Church's predominant influence, and Johann Georg Jacobi became the first Protestant professor in 1784.</p><p>Grand Duke Ludwig I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> rescued the university with an annual contribution in 1820, and it has since been named the Albert Ludwig University. In 1900 it became the first German university to accept women. Under the Nazi dictatorship it went through political alignment; under the rector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-heidegger">Martin Heidegger</a> all Jewish faculty members were forced to leave. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it reopened, and the Freiburg School of economists, among them Walter Eucken and Franz Böhm, developed the ordoliberal ideas that influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s social market economy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The university seal, in use since 1462, shows Christ on a gothic throne offering the gospel to Jewish scholars, with the arms of the Austrian duchies, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and the city of Freiburg. Its scholars have included Edmund Husserl, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-heidegger">Martin Heidegger</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannah-arendt">Hannah Arendt</a>, Edith Stein, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-weber">Max Weber</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-hayek">Friedrich Hayek</a> and Hans Spemann. The Jugendstil Kollegiengebäude I, built in 1911 by Hermann Billing, stands in the historic centre, and the university has offered German language courses to foreign students since 1911.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “University of Freiburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
